introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do we build software that mirrors the real world?"
  overview: "This unit marks a paradigm shift from 'doing' (procedural functions) to 'being' (object-oriented programming). You will learn to model software around <strong>Objects</strong> — distinct entities that combine data (attributes) and behavior (methods) into reusable blueprints called <strong>Classes</strong>."
  journey: "You'll start by distinguishing between a <strong>Class</strong> (the blueprint) and an <strong>Object</strong> (the house). Then, you'll master <strong>UML diagrams</strong> to design these structures, learn to control access with <strong>Encapsulation</strong>, and investigate the difference between data that belongs to an object vs data that belongs to the class (<strong>Static</strong>)."

concepts:
  - title: "Classes & Objects"
    icon: "fa-cube"
    content: |
      <p>The core philosophy of OOP is modeled on reality:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Class:</strong> The architectural blueprint or template (e.g., "Car" design).</li>
          <li><strong>Object:</strong> A specific instance built from that blueprint (e.g., "My Red Toyota").</li>
          <li><strong>Instantiation:</strong> The process of creating an object from a class.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "UML Diagrams"
    icon: "fa-project-diagram"
    content: |
      <p>Before coding, we design using <strong>Unified Modeling Language (UML)</strong> class diagrams. They have 3 standard sections:</p>
      <ol>
          <li><strong>Class Name:</strong> (e.g., <code>BankAccount</code>)</li>
          <li><strong>Attributes (Fields):</strong> Data the object knows (e.g., <code>- balance: double</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Methods (Actions):</strong> Things the object does (e.g., <code>+ deposit(amount)</code>).</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Note symbols: <code>+</code> means Public, <code>-</code> means Private.</p>

  - title: "Encapsulation"
    icon: "fa-shield-halved"
    content: |
      <p>Good objects hide their internal workings. <strong>Encapsulation</strong> restricts direct access to data ('private variables') and forces external code to use <strong>Accessor (Get)</strong> and <strong>Mutator (Set)</strong> methods.</p>
      <div class="warning-box">
          <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> Why Encapsulate?</h4>
          <p>It protects data integrity. A <code>setAge(-5)</code> method can reject invalid data, whereas direct access <code>p.age = -5</code> cannot.</p>
      </div>

  - title: "Static vs Instance"
    icon: "fa-sitemap"
    content: |
      <p>Not all data belongs to a specific object:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Instance Variable:</strong> Unique to each object (e.g., <code>myScore</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Static Variable:</strong> Shared by ALL objects of that class (e.g., <code>totalPlayers</code>).</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Object-Oriented Programming"
    definition: "A paradigm based on the concept of 'objects', which contain data and code: data in the form of fields, and code in the form of procedures."
  - term: "Class"
    definition: "A blueprint or template for creating objects."
  - term: "Object (Instance)"
    definition: "A specific realization of a class, created in memory."
  - term: "Constructor (__init__)"
    definition: "A special method called when an object is instantiated to initialize its attributes."
  - term: "Attribute"
    definition: "A variable that belongs to an object or class."
  - term: "Method"
    definition: "A function that belongs to an object or class."
  - term: "Encapsulation"
    definition: "Bundling data and methods that work on that data within one unit, and restricting access to some of the object's components."
  - term: "Accessor (Getter)"
    definition: "A method used to return the value of a private attribute."
  - term: "Mutator (Setter)"
    definition: "A method used to update the value of a private attribute."
  - term: "Static"
    definition: "A keyword indicating a member belongs to the class itself, not to instances of the class."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Procedural Habits"
    correction: "Trying to write all logic outside the class or passing every variable as a parameter. Trust the object's internal state (<code>self.variable</code>)!"
  - mistake: "Private = Encryption"
    correction: "Believing private variables are 'secure'. They are just hidden from accidental misuse (naming convention), not encrypted."
  - mistake: "One Static for All"
    correction: "Confusion over why changing a static variable affects everyone. e.g. If you change the 'InterestRate', it changes for EVERY bank account."

